Title: THE PARALLAX DINNER
Credit: Written by
Author: Adapted from the Animated Series Bible
Draft date: October 21, 2026
Contact: Wiregrass Basin Production Unit

===

EXT. DIXIE-STARLIGHT TRUCK STOP - NIGHT

A relentless South Georgia squall drums against miles of oil-slicked asphalt. 

Parked with clinical, laser-calibrated precision across four empty stalls sits an armored, blindingly white vintage Airstream trailer. Its polished aluminum shell reflects the flickering neon signs in pure, undisturbed rectilinear lines.

Twenty feet away, idling in neutral, looms an eighty-thousand-pound Peterbilt 379. Its massive Cummins diesel rumbles in low, arrhythmic cardiac thuds, swaying rhythmically on worn leaf-spring suspension--a hulking, mud-caked pachyderm of steel and lithium grease.

INT. DIXIE-STARLIGHT DINER - CONTINUOUS

Fluorescent tubes hum at sixty hertz overhead. 

At a corner booth, DR. ORBIE MAYHAW sits locked in the Euclidean Posture. Her spine forms an unbending vertical y-axis against the red vinyl; her shoulders are a dead-level perpendicular crossbar, resting at a strict ninety-degree angle to the table.

She wears an immaculate, starched white cotton-twill optometrist's coat fastened by asymmetrical ceramic buttons over a high-collared shirt and a narrow black four-in-hand tie. Round black-acetate frames support flip-down articulated loupes over her eyes. 

Tight white cotton gloves cover her hands. With microscopic precision, she slides a stainless-steel drafting square along the formica, nudging a water glass and a paper napkin until they align to exact right angles. She smells of pure isopropyl alcohol, glass cleaner, and grinding-wheel ozone.

The diner's double doors swing wide with a hydraulic hiss.

JOHNNY WALKMAN enters.

He moves with the Elephant's Sway--weight centered deep in his hips and gut, rolling side-to-side like a freight vessel navigating heavy swell. He wears oil-caked denim, a heavy buffalo-plaid flannel with the sleeves rolled over thick, sun-baked forearms, and steel-toed boots dusted with white limestone. A military-grade boom-mic headset is clamped over his right ear, its standby LED pulsing a steady, gentle green. 

He smells of unburned diesel, crushed wet pine needles, stale chicory coffee, and orange Gojo hand cleaner.

Johnny spots Orbie. His face opens into a wide, radiant, profoundly sincere smile, his eyes liquid and unblinking. 

JOHNNY
(voice a warm, rapid-fire radio baritone)
Doctor Mayhaw. Look at you. You're an absolute Cartesian monument sitting inside a sea of cholesterol and ungrounded wiring. Channel nineteen had two haulers swearing there was an apparition in booth four. I told them on the squelch, no boys, that's just the high priestess of geometric hygiene having supper with an unregenerate gear-jammer.

Johnny slides into the opposing bench. His massive bulk tilts the booth on its rusted floor bolts, throwing the table two degrees off level. 

Orbie's jaw tightens. Her left eye tracks the displaced drafting square.

ORBIE
(cadence of a geometric proof)
You are three minutes late, Mr. Walkman. And your mass has introduced a seven-millimeter yaw into the horizontal plane of this table.

JOHNNY
(chuckling warmly)
That ain't yaw, Orbie, that's the Elephant taking a seat. The chassis settles where the gravity tells it to settle. 

A weary WAITRESS drops two laminated menus sticky with syrup onto the table. Johnny catches her eye immediately, his hand lightly brushing the air between them in an open, welcoming gesture.

JOHNNY (CONT'D)
Evening, Miss Beverly. You been on your feet since four o'clock, haven't you? That third burner on the flat-top has a bad thermocouple--I heard it ticking when I walked past the grease trap. You don't have to haul that heavy pot over here; just pour whatever's left in the bottom of the decanter into two mugs, and we'll call it a righteous tithe to the shift.

The Waitress stares at him, disarmed, her shoulders dropping three inches of tension. She nods gratefully and retreats.

ORBIE
You just bypassed standard operational protocol to construct an emotional feedback loop.

JOHNNY
I acknowledged the Care foundation, Orbie. Haidt 101. She's working twelve hours to float a mortgage in a swamp that's flooding under corporate timber liens. If I treat her like an automated vending machine, I'm participating in the degradation of the collective hive. Mitsein, darling. Being-with.

ORBIE
She is a commercial service provider in a contractual transaction. By projecting your intrusive communal empathy onto her fatigue, you have blurred the boundary between her labor and your guilt. 

Orbie reaches into her coat pocket with gloved fingers. She pulls out an individually wrapped isopropyl wipe, tears it open with a sharp rip, and methodically scrubs the plastic laminate of her menu.

ORBIE (CONT'D)
You live in the blur, Johnny. Your truck lists to the left. Your windshield is clouded with bug grease and dried surfactant. And you drive eighty thousand pounds of kinetic slaughter through blind sloughs while chattering over a microphone to convince yourself that chaos is just complicated love.

JOHNNY
(leaning in, forearms on the table)
And you sit inside an armored silver capsule running diamond grinding wheels on polycarbonate, convincing yourself that if you can just get every sharecropper's astigmatism corrected to twenty-fifteen, nobody will ever swing a tire iron at his brother again.

Orbie freezes. Her fingers pause on the wipe.

ORBIE
Visual astigmatism is the physical root of ethical relativism. A man whose retinal image is warped cannot perceive the unbending line of a contract. The basin's warlords do not rule by strength; they rule because their victims see the world in comfortable, forgiving, myopic smears. When I clamp a plus-four lens in front of a man's pupil, I do not offer him comfort. I offer him the geometry of his actual condition.

JOHNNY
And they throw up on your floorboards, don't they?

Orbie's breath catches--subtle, nasal, rigid.

ORBIE
Vertigo is the natural biological tax of truth.

JOHNNY
Vertigo is what happens when you strip the rider's saddle off before the elephant is ready to stop running. You think humans are compasses and protractors, Orbie. You think if you dial in the diopter, the soul lines up like a picket fence. But human beings are ninety percent chimp and ten percent bee. We need the blur. The blur is the grease that keeps the universal joints from seizing up when the highway gets rutted out.

ORBIE
The blur is cowardice.

Orbie reaches into a fitted leather interior pocket of her white twill coat. She withdraws a brass-and-black-steel trial lens frame. She sets it on the formica between them like a challenge duel.

ORBIE (CONT'D)
Put them on.

Johnny looks down at the contraption with wide, liquid eyes. He smiles faintly.

JOHNNY
Now why would I want to look through your cold little iron windows?

ORBIE
Because you look at this greasy room through the cataract of your own relentless benevolence. Put them on, Mr. Walkman. Let me refract your Rider. Let me show you what the Starlight Diner actually looks like without your CB radio translating the decay into folklore.

Johnny chuckles, low and resonant, but his jaw sets. He takes off his sweat-stained mesh cap, setting it beside his water glass. 

He lifts the heavy trial frames. He hooks the cold curved wire temples over his ears, tucking them around his boom-mic headset.

Orbie doesn't blink. She reaches across the table with her white cotton gloves, selecting two circular glass discs from a brass velvet-lined case on her lap. 

She slots a high-cylinder lens into the right eye with a decisive, metallic *CLICK*. Then the left.

ORBIE (CONT'D)
Axial rotation: one hundred and fifteen degrees. Axis zeroed. Cylinder: minus two-point-seven-five. Look at the counter, Johnny. Look past the pie case.

Johnny looks.

The world Snaps into razor-edged, merciless focus.

The warm, amber glow of the diner vanishes, replaced by the naked, violent exposure of forty-watt fluorescent daylight. 

He sees the colony of black mold crawling in microscopic clusters along the silicone gasket of the pie display. He sees the hairline stress fracture running down Miss Beverly's swollen right ankle through her stocking. He sees the grease film floating atop the sugar dispenser--thick, grey, stagnant.

Johnny's breath stutters. His massive shoulders hitch. 

A visceral, biological wave of Disgust hits his throat--the Haidt Sanctity foundation violently tripped by unbuffered microbial rot. He swallows hard, his gut churning.

ORBIE (CONT'D)
(quiet, mathematical cadence)
Tell me what you see on channel nineteen now. Tell me how the highway binds us when you can see the asbestos shedding from the acoustic ceiling tiles directly into the butter dish. It is not a hive, Johnny. It is an unmaintained abattoir of decaying carbon.

Johnny sits frozen under the heavy frames. For five seconds, the frantic, folksy Rider in his head goes utterly, completely silent. 

There is no broadcast. No Haidt axles. Just the raw, terrifying, freezing void of unfiltered physical reality.

Orbie watches him, her chin held level, waiting for the collapse.

Then--Johnny's chest rises. A slow, massive inhalation of diesel fumes and chicory.

He reaches up. He doesn't take the trial frames off. 

Instead, he slowly, deliberately turns his head to look directly at Orbie through the high-diopter lenses.

Through the flawless optical glass, he sees her.

Not the unyielding geometric vanguard. He sees the frayed, microscopic threads at the edge of her starched collar where bleach has eaten the cotton. He sees the tremor in her left eyelid, pulsing at irregular intervals. He sees the faint, faded shadow of a red clay smudge behind her ear that no amount of isopropyl alcohol could ever scrub away.

His eyes pool with wet, invading, terrifyingly deep pity.

JOHNNY
(voice dropped to an intimate, devastating murmur)
God almighty, Orbie. You're freezing to death in there, aren't you?

Orbie's rigid posture flinches fractionally.

ORBIE
Do not attempt psychological projection. Read the chart.

JOHNNY
You didn't build that trailer out of aluminum because you love Euclid. You built it because when you were a little girl, the mud came right up to the porch, didn't it? The swamp doesn't have right angles. The swamp grows in curves and rotting loops, and it swallowed something of yours, and you swore you'd put the whole county between two parallel lines so it couldn't ever touch you again.

ORBIE
(voice tightening, alto pitch rising)
Parallel lines are the only absolute truth in this continent. They never meet, Mr. Walkman. They do not betray one another.

JOHNNY
They don't touch, either.

Johnny reaches his right hand across the table. 

He doesn't snatch. He executes the Heavy Reach--slow, warm, inevitable, grease-stained palm turned open toward the heavens. 

His thick fingers stop two inches from her starched cuff. He doesn't invade; he offers the ground.

JOHNNY (CONT'D)
You spend all day making people look at their own ruin through twenty-fifteen glass. But you wear cotton gloves so you don't ever have to feel the temperature of the skin underneath. You think you're saving them from the blur? You're just giving them an autopsy report while they're still breathing.

Orbie looks down at his open palm.

The lithium grease on his knuckles forms an irregular, non-Euclidean smear against his scarred skin. Her breath turns shallow. Her fingers twitch inside the cotton inspection gloves.

ORBIE
If I touch that grease, the lens grinder in my shop will carry the contaminant for three weeks.

JOHNNY
Then we'll wash it off together with Gojo and hot tap water. I'll turn the handles.

ORBIE
You cannot wash away the structural inaccuracy of this county with orange pumice soap.

JOHNNY
I'll make you a deal, Doctor Mayhaw. A bilateral treaty across the axle.

He points a blunt, scarred forefinger to the trial frames on his nose.

JOHNNY (CONT'D)
I'll keep these damn glasses on for the rest of supper. I'll look at the mold. I'll look at the rust on the sugar shakers. I won't broadcast a single word of justification on the radio. I'll sit right here in the freezing high-definition dirt with you.

ORBIE
And what is my required concession in this transaction?

Johnny reaches up with his other hand. He taps the blinking green LED on his boom headset.

JOHNNY
You take off the gloves. And I unclip the mic.

Silence hangs between them, heavier than forty tons of wet river timber.

Outside, a logging semi roars past on the blacktop, its headlights dragging a long, skewed quadrilateral of yellow light across the diner ceiling.

Orbie looks from the green diode on his headset down to her pristine white cotton palms. 

She knows the mathematics. If she removes the gloves, the clinical sterile barrier of the Airstream is breached. The boundary condition dissolves. The blur enters the laboratory.

And if Johnny unplugs his radio, the frantic Rider on his shoulder falls off into the mud, leaving him alone in the dark with an eighty-thousand-pound heart he doesn't know how to steer.

ORBIE
(whisper, crisp as dry paper)
That is an asymmetric trade. It violates the principle of equivalent exchange.

JOHNNY
(smiling, soft and heartbreakingly steady)
That's why they call it a date, Orbie. It ain't an invoice.

Orbie's jaw works once. 

Slowly--with the agonizing, microscopic deliberation of a vault door unsealing--her right hand reaches across to the wrist of her left glove. 

Her gloved thumb hooks the hem. 

She peels it back two inches. 

Pale, bare, human skin meets the humid, grease-scented air of the Wiregrass night.

Johnny doesn't wait. His hand moves to his right ear. 

He slips the boom-mic headset off his head. The green light blinks out. He sets the communication rig squarely on top of the laminated menu.

He reaches out his bare, scarred hand.

Orbie hesitates for half a second--then lays her bare fingers into the warm, rough center of his palm.

The diner booth sways. The fluorescent lights flicker.

Neither of them looks away.

> FADE OUT.